{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795606153,"nonce":"uzdSSNWO5hhxI0ByIHOhrDtWWoI=","title":"Headless frames do not tick scroll physics; the window does","message":"`Headless::frame` applies pending `ScrollRef` requests but never calls `RenderTree::tick_scroll_physics`, which the windowed frame loop calls each frame. So in headless tests momentum, edge springs, scrollbar fades and any spring-driven scroll (`scroll_to_animated`, used by scroll-to-element and now by smooth `scroll_by` / `set_scroll_offset`) stand still.\n\nFound while making the rich text editor's toolbar scroll buttons ease (26002b452b75be9fe98ed2910cd304adffc0646930cfabb9a4f22c9e719c598b). Ticking unconditionally in `Headless::frame` broke five recorder tests that were written against the non-ticking harness:\n\n- cn_select_snapshots::a_long_combobox_list_stays_inside_its_popup\n- animation_paths::a_fading_scrollbar_over_still_content_takes_the_fast_path\n- animation_paths::a_raised_panel_stays_above_a_fading_scrollbar\n- animation_paths::the_node_table_against_the_reference\n- animation_paths::the_cpu_rows_against_the_reference\n\nFor now the tick is opt-in: `Headless::scroll_physics()`. The toolbar scroll test turns it on.\n\nTo close: check each of the five against the window's behaviour, update them (or the goldens) to the ticking harness, and make ticking the default so headless matches the window.","files":null}]}